    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Savarkar</a> defined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> as one who regards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> to the seas, as both fatherland and holy land, and distinguished Hindutva, a national and cultural identity, from Hinduism as a religion.</li><li>From the RSS, founded in 1925 after Hedgewar met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Savarkar</a>, grew the Sangh Parivar, including the Vishva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Parishad (1964) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a> (1980), which adopted Hindutva as its ideology in 1989.</li><li>Its nature is disputed: some scholars call it fascist or near-fascist, others an extreme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> or ethnic nationalism, while the BJP calls it cultural nationalism and not a religious or theocratic concept.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Savarkar</a>, an atheist, opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system: under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a> held all-caste dinners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and Kanpur, and he gave parents money so that their children of the so-called lower castes would attend school.</p></aside>
    </section>

